module Core

# ============================================================================
# D64Trans — decimal64 (Pmax=16) transcendentals + libm surface via B128: every
# transcendental converts D64 -> B128, evaluates there, and rounds B128 -> D64 once
# (16 digits). Where B128 has a correctly rounded CORE-MATH routine (exp, exp2, exp10,
# expm1, log, sin, cos, asin, acos, atan, atan2, cbrt, sqrt, hypot) the D64 result is
# wrong only when the true value lies within ~2^-113 of a decimal midpoint; the rest run
# on the B128 engine (~34 digits). The DD helpers below remain for D32 and conversions.
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# Radix adapters (dec <-> DD). A D64 value is coeff * 10^exp; DD holds hi+lo on
# hardware b64. Conversion is exact for the coefficient (a <=16-digit integer split
# into two <=8-digit halves, each < 2^53 so b64-exact) and faithful for the 10^exp
# scale (a two-level sqrt-split power-of-ten table, ~1 ULP of DD).
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# Power-of-ten DD tables (sqrt-split, S=20). Four small tables of DD (hi,lo) pairs stored as their
# raw IEEE bit patterns (exact, no literal-parse rounding): HP=10^(20*hi) hi=0..20; LP=10^lo lo=0..19;
# HN=10^-(20*hi); LN=10^-lo. dd_pow10(e) = HP[|e|/20]*LP[|e|%20] (or HN/LN for e<0) — one DD multiply,
# zero division. Generated at ~120-digit precision, round-half-even; entries beyond b64's range are
# stored as +inf (10^320+) or 0 (10^-340-), matching the old binary-exp overflow/underflow boundary.
# ============================================================================
